::ENG2_U06_L04_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide heading best states a finding?{
=A. Many Students Remain on Campus After Dismissal#Correct. This heading tells the audience what the evidence shows.
~B. Survey#Incorrect. This names a source type but not the finding.
~C. Stuff About School#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~D. Slide Two#Incorrect. This labels position, not meaning.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.05] Which visual feature best helps the audience compare survey numbers?{
~A. A busy background image.#Incorrect. This distracts from the numbers.
=B. A simple labeled bar chart.#Correct. A bar chart helps the audience compare quantities.
~C. Moving text on every line.#Incorrect. This can distract the audience.
~D. A decorative border with no labels.#Incorrect. Decoration does not clarify the evidence.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which slide text is most concise and explanatory?{
~A. There are lots of things and students and places and the idea is important.#Incorrect. This is wordy and unclear.
~B. Stuff matters.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=C. Supervised study space turns waiting time into safe academic time.#Correct. This is concise and explanatory.
~D. The slide has a chart and colors.#Incorrect. This describes the slide rather than explaining the reasoning.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Why should slides avoid full paragraphs of speech text?{
~A. Full paragraphs always prove the claim better.#Incorrect. Long slide text often overloads the audience.
~B. The speaker should never explain anything.#Incorrect. The speaker should explain the slide.
~C. Evidence should be hidden from the audience.#Incorrect. Evidence should be clear.
=D. Slides should support the speaker with concise points, not replace the speaker.#Correct. Slides guide audience understanding while the speaker explains.
}

::ENG2_U06_L04_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.C.02] Which slide sequence is clearest?{
~A. Conclusion, random image, evidence, topic.#Incorrect. This sequence is confusing.
=B. Finding, evidence, reasoning, conclusion.#Correct. This sequence builds understanding logically.
~C. Decoration, decoration, decoration, closing.#Incorrect. Decoration does not communicate findings.
~D. Evidence only, with no reasoning or conclusion.#Incorrect. The audience needs reasoning and closure.
}
